# Typography: orphans & runts

Avoid unbalanced line breaks in all project copy unless the user opts out (`.allow-runts` / `[data-allow-runts]`).

## What to prevent
- A **short word left at the end of a line** when the rest of its phrase continues below (e.g. dangling “I” before “lead…”).
- A **one-word (or very short) last line** of a paragraph (e.g. lone “why?”) — a **runt** / widow-like break.

## How to fix (prefer in this order)
1. Keep `text-wrap: pretty` on the document (already on `body`).
2. Use a non-breaking space so the fragment stays with its phrase: `I&nbsp;lead`, `and&nbsp;why?`, `now&nbsp;AI`.
3. Rely on the site’s `preventRunts()` script for prose (`p`, `q`, headings, `.lede`, etc.) — do not remove it without a replacement.
4. For new copy, write or mark up so short sentence-starters and short endings don’t strand alone; glue with `&nbsp;` when a break is known-bad at the design width.

## Exceptions
Only skip this when the user explicitly allows runts, or marks the node with `.allow-runts` / `data-allow-runts`.
